      "lineNbr": 680,
      "author": {
        "id": 1441652
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-01-20T16:09:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s a general problem in `fpsensor` architecture. The most notable example is `sensor_mode` which is changed both from `fpsensor` and `hostcmd` tasks, without even being atomic 😊\n\nThis works because:\n- Host commands have higher priority than `fpsensor` task.\n- Our MCUs are single-core.\n- There could be only one host command running at the time.\n- Host commands cannot be interrupted by other tasks.\n- We are checking only one bit in `global_context.sensor_mode`, so we are not prone to partial (non-atomic) writes/reads.\n\nIMHO this CL does not introduce any new risks. The best option would be to rework our codebase to use message passing.",
      "parentUuid": "25ce576b_c1931b85",
      "revId": "6a9eedbdc799103cd985b1d7aeb5198dd3b7f257",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
